A hash map stores key-value pairs by hashing the key to an integer and using it to pick a bucket. Collisions — different keys landing in the same bucket — are resolved by chaining (a list per bucket) or open addressing (probing the next slot).

```python
class HashMap:
    def __init__(self, size=16):
        self.size = size
        self.buckets = [[] for _ in range(size)]

    def put(self, key, val):
        b = self.buckets[hash(key) % self.size]
        for i, (k, _) in enumerate(b):
            if k == key:
                b[i] = (key, val); return
        b.append((key, val))

    def get(self, key):
        for k, v in self.buckets[hash(key) % self.size]:
            if k == key:
                return v
        raise KeyError(key)
```
